### Ticket

`<WO-ID>` - `<full title>`. Position `<n>` of `<total>`.
Predecessor `<WO-ID>` - `<full title>`, merged, closed and archived.

### What just landed

What the previous ticket actually put on `main`, in enough detail that the reader
does not have to go and diff it. Name the files and the shapes, not the commits.

### What is NOT done

The single most valuable section, and the one most often written dishonestly.
State plainly what was left unbuilt, what was never applied, and what an
acceptance criterion recorded as unmet. If nothing reached a real system, say so
in those words and say what proves it - a command and its empty output.

Anything carried onto a different ticket is named here by ID and full title, so
it cannot be quietly dropped.

### Stale or false in the docs

Every place the specification, the runbook or an older ticket now says something
untrue, with the correction. A reader who trusts a stale doc loses a day.

### Your scope

What this ticket builds. Where the files go, and where they explicitly do not go
when two similar things already exist.

### Before you start

Prerequisites and anything unsettled that must be answered before work begins.
If a question here has no answer, the agent asks the user rather than choosing
one and building on it.

Write `None.` when there is genuinely nothing. An empty section is
indistinguishable from a forgotten one.

### Read in this order

An ordered list, not a pile of links. Start with `CLAUDE.md`, then
`CONTEXT_STATE.md` top 10 checkpoints, then the specification sections that
apply, then the ticket file, then any external runbook and the exact steps of it.

### Reuse, it is proven

The components that already work, what they take, and the sharp edge on each.
This is where a reader learns not to rewrite something that exists.

### The verification ladder

How to prove the work without burning a real run per attempt. Cheapest rung
first. Name what each rung has caught before, so it reads as evidence rather
than ceremony.

### Traps, already paid for

One line each: the symptom first, then the cause. The symptom is what a reader
recognises at the moment it happens; the cause is only useful afterwards.

### Workflow

The literal commands for approve, start, evidence, submit, done and close, with
the project's own paths filled in. Copy-paste, not description.

### Conventions

The house rules that apply to every reply and every commit: ticket references
carry ID and full title, plain-language reporting, no agent co-author lines, no
em dashes, failing tests reported as failing.
